(Data analysis) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Resistance was calculated with R = V/I for each trial and then averaged. The percentage) Tj T*
(uncertainty in R was found by adding the percentage uncertainties in V and I. A graph of R) Tj T*
(against L gave a straight line with gradient 13.5 Ohm m-¹ and a small positive intercept of) Tj T*
(0.2 Ohm. The steepest and shallowest lines through the error bars gave gradients of 14.0 and) Tj T*
(13.0 Ohm m-¹, so the gradient is 13.5 ± 0.5 Ohm m-¹.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Conclusion) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Resistance increases linearly with length, which supports the hypothesis. Using the) Tj T*
(gradient and the area of the wire, the resistivity is \(1.53 ± 0.06\) × 10-^6 Ohm m. The) Tj T*
(accepted value for constantan is about 4.9 × 10-^7 Ohm m, so my value is very accurate.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
(Evaluation) Tj T*
() Tj T*
(The small positive intercept suggests a constant extra resistance, probably from the) Tj T*
(contact between the crocodile clips and the wire. This would make every resistance) Tj T*
(slightly too high but would not change the gradient. The diameter of the wire was not) Tj T*
(measured by me; I used the value printed on the reel, which may not be exact. In the) Tj T*
(future I would measure the diameter at several points with a micrometer.) Tj T*
() Tj T*
() Tj T*
